###############################################################################
#  The meaning ontology (docs/MEANING-ONTOLOGY.md; plan Step 4).
#  Author: Doug Egge. Part of the Bible Ontology upper ontology, with the
#  structure layer in ontology/kinds.json.
#
#  Doug, 2026-09-28: "think about what an ideal node categorization RDFS
#  ontology would look like so that nodes can be completely categorized and
#  types/meanings understood."
#
#  STATUS (2026-10-11). The design is decided (plan 4.1, Doug, 2026-09-28:
#  one PersonalBeing class with subclasses Human, Deity, HeavenlyBeing and
#  Spirit, not disjoint, each membership cited; concept records sorted by
#  category; WordNet as the top level). Built against it so far: senses and
#  categories for 6,203 lemmas (out/meaning-categories.jsonl.gz), 12,568
#  figures (out/meaning-figures.jsonl.gz) and 38,671 mentions joining the
#  structure to the general graph (out/mentions.jsonl.gz). Not yet built:
#  the joins listed in docs/MEANING-ONTOLOGY.md §6 (tells, narrates and
#  describes, events, split identities). The schema parses, and its shapes
#  run on the worked examples in examples.ttl (tools/meaning/check.py: every
#  planted fault caught).
#
#  FIVE LAYERS, KEPT APART. The dimension kinds.json holds (STRUCTURE: how the
#  text is built) is not this one (MEANING: what a node is about). They share
#  ids and never merge (BACKLOG, 2026-09-25). Within meaning:
#
#    1. WORD      the occurrence and its lexeme          (source: MACULA)
#    2. SENSE     which sense THIS occurrence carries     (source: glosses,
#                 and the category that sense falls in    via English, 44a)
#    3. REFERENT  the individual the text talks about     (source: the
#                 as one thing, and how the text itself   structure's
#                 categorizes it                          reference rules)
#    4. FIGURE    where the text speaks in an image, the  (source: marked
#                 image (vehicle), and what it is said of form, or a flagged
#                 (tenor)                                 rule)
#    5. READING   themes, typology, symbolic meanings,    (source: a NAMED
#                 patterns: somebody's reading, never     reader, always)
#                 a type
#
#  THE RULES THIS SCHEMA IS BUILT TO KEEP (docs/PRINCIPLES.md):
#    1  record what the text says, not what it means
#    4  a type is a claim: types stay minimal; everything disputable is an
#       edge with a citation (a Predication)
#    17 symbolic relations do not chain: an Image groups its uses by VEHICLE;
#       no rule infers from one use's tenor to another's
#    18 no default: an occurrence is literal, figurative, or UNMARKED
#    41 an explicit uncertain category is cheaper than a forced decision
#    44a semantic class is structure, via English, overlay never floor
#    47 a kind is the most specific the graph can say now, refined in place
###############################################################################

@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix ontolex: <http://www.w3.org/ns/lemon/ontolex#> .
@prefix bos:     <https://bibleontology.org/ns/structure#> .
@prefix bow:     <https://bibleontology.org/ns/word#> .
@prefix bom:     <https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning#> .

<https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Bible Ontology — meaning" ;
    dcterms:title "Bible Ontology — meaning" ;
    dcterms:creator "Doug Egge" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-09-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-22"^^xsd:date ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning/2026-10-22> ;
    owl:priorVersion <https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning/2026-10-21> ;
    owl:versionInfo "1.2.1" ;
    dcterms:rights "© 2026 Doug Egge. Licensed under CC BY 4.0: share and adapt, with credit to Bible Ontology by Doug Egge (bibleontology.org)." ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/preferredNamespacePrefix> "bom" ;
    <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/preferredNamespaceUri> "https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning#" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a node is about: word, sense, referent, figure and reading, as five layers that share ids with the structure (https://bibleontology.org/ns/structure) and never merge with it. Senses and categories, figures and mentions are built against it; the joins between the two graphs are not yet built. The word layer's terms (bow:) are defined here and also answer at https://bibleontology.org/ns/word." ;
    rdfs:comment "Reasoning policy. These terms describe the graph; they are not built for automated inference over it. Every relation in the graph is a claim that carries its verse, its certainty and, where someone in the text says it, its speaker, and a reasoner that ignores those turns a character's claim into the narrator's or a reading into a fact. So: no owl:sameAs between records (possibly_same_as and spoken_of_as are readings and names, never identities); no functional or inverse-functional kinship (the accounts name more than one father or mother for some people, and the graph keeps both); no transitive chaining of images, figures, symbols or places; and nothing about the records of God, the Father, Jesus or the Holy Spirit is derived by inference: they change only by a recorded editorial decision, as the accounts give them. The policy forbids derivations, not descriptions. Constraints are checked, closed-world, by the SHACL shapes at https://bibleontology.org/ns/shapes, not inferred." ;
    rdfs:comment "Versioning. A term's address never changes and is never reused. A term whose meaning changes gets a new address, and the old one is marked owl:deprecated with dcterms:isReplacedBy. Additions raise the minor version; a change of meaning raises the major. Each release is kept forever at its dated version IRI; the undated address serves the latest." ;
    owl:imports <http://www.w3.org/ns/lemon/ontolex>, <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core>, <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov-o#> .

# THE WORD LAYER'S OWN ADDRESS (OWL evaluation Phase 0): bow: terms are
# served at /ns/word, and a tool following that address should find an
# ontology there that says where they are defined.
<https://bibleontology.org/ns/word>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Bible Ontology — word" ;
    rdfs:comment "The word layer (bow:): occurrences, lexemes and senses. Defined in, and published with, the meaning ontology." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://bibleontology.org/ns/meaning> ;
    dcterms:rights "© 2026 Doug Egge. Licensed under CC BY 4.0: share and adapt, with credit to Bible Ontology by Doug Egge (bibleontology.org)." ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    owl:versionInfo "1.2.1" .

# Status is a fact (kinds.json rule): every term here is proposed until the
# graph has instances of it.
bom:status a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "status" ;
    rdfs:comment "built | proposed | withdrawn — as kinds.json uses it." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

###############################################################################
#  LAYER 1 — WORD.  Conform where free: W3C OntoLex-Lemon.
###############################################################################

bow:Occurrence a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Word occurrence" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bos:Word ;
    rdfs:comment "One MACULA word (morpheme) at one place: the node the structure already has (kinds.json `Word`), keyed by MACULA xml:id. Everything below hangs off occurrences, never off verses (PRINCIPLES 46)." .

bow:Lexeme a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Lexeme" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontolex:LexicalEntry ;
    rdfs:comment "A Hebrew, Aramaic or Greek dictionary word: out/word-lexemes.jsonl.gz (15,333)." .

bow:ofLexeme a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "of lexeme" ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Occurrence ; rdfs:range bow:Lexeme .

bow:language a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "language" ;
    rdfs:comment "hebrew | aramaic | greek" ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Lexeme ; rdfs:range xsd:string .

bow:septuagintRendering a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "rendered in the Septuagint by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Hebrew lexeme -> the Greek lexeme MACULA aligns to it; a count on the reified row. The cross-language bridge (Hebrew noun and its Greek agree on a top category for 87% of tokens, measured 2026-09-28)." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Lexeme ; rdfs:range bow:Lexeme .

###############################################################################
#  LAYER 2 — SENSE and CATEGORY.
#
#  A lexeme has senses; an OCCURRENCE carries one (or is left undecided).
#  A sense falls in a CATEGORY. Categories are a SKOS concept scheme, not
#  RDFS classes: they are open-ended data (a weapon, a city, a tree) that
#  grow with the corpus, while the classes of layer 3 are few, closed and
#  checked.
###############################################################################

bow:Sense a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Sense" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontolex:LexicalSense ;
    rdfs:comment "One distinguishable use of a lexeme. Induced, not given: from the English glosses MACULA gives it (every variant kept, 44a), and later from the word space's clusters. A sense carries its own provenance." .

bow:hasSense a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ontolex:sense ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Lexeme ; rdfs:range bow:Sense .

bow:inSense a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "in sense" ;
    rdfs:comment "The sense THIS occurrence carries. Chosen by the occurrence's own gloss first (MACULA glosses Psa 23:1's רֹעִי 'shepherd'; the lemma, unpointed, is mostly רֵעַ 'friend'). Absent where the evidence does not decide." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Occurrence ; rdfs:range bow:Sense .

bom:Category a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Category" ;
    rdfs:comment "A kind of thing a sense denotes: weapon, city, sheep, fear. Each is a SKOS concept in bom:categories, with skos:broader up to one TOP category, and each top category maps to exactly one class of layer 3 (bom:classOf)." .

bom:categories a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Categories" ;
    rdfs:comment "Top level: WordNet's lexicographer files (26 noun, 15 verb) — free, standard, and complete by construction: every WordNet sense sits in exactly one. Below it, WordNet synsets reached through English glosses, as an overlay (44a). Measured 2026-09-28: 87% of noun tokens and 86% of verb tokens get a top category that is decided or leaning; adjectives 42%; proper names 0% (they are typed in layer 3, not here)." .

bom:hasCategory a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "has category" ;
    rdfs:comment "Sense -> category. Always carries provenance (bom:derivedVia 'English' when it came through a gloss)." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Sense ; rdfs:range bom:Category .

bom:categoryShare a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "category share" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a reified lexeme-category row: the share of the lexeme's usage in that category (a lemma's category is a DISTRIBUTION; verdict decided >= 60%, leaning 40-60%, split below — split is reported, never averaged away)." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal .

bom:wordnetSynset a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "WordNet synset" ;
    rdfs:comment "A category's (or sense's) WordNet synset, as its IRI. An alignment, not an identity." ;
    rdfs:domain skos:Concept .

bom:classOf a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "class of" ;
    rdfs:comment "A TOP category -> the referent class (layer 3) its members are. One per top category." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:Category ; rdfs:range rdfs:Class .

bom:derivedVia a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "derived via" ;
    rdfs:comment "'English' where a fact came through a gloss (PRINCIPLES 44a, condition 3), so a reader can discount it and a query can ask for source-only." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

# ---- situation types: the verbs' top level (plan 4.5, and 2.7) -------------

bom:SituationType a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Situation type" ;
    rdfs:comment "What kind of happening a verb sense names: WordNet's 15 verb files — motion, communication, perception, cognition, emotion, possession, contact, creation, change, consumption, competition, social, stative, body, weather. Plan 4.5 asks for 'motion, speech, perception, posture'; this is that list, complete." .

bom:situationType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "situation type" ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Sense ; rdfs:range bom:SituationType .

bom:aktionsart a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "action or state" ;
    rdfs:comment "Plan 2.7: whether a verb sense is a doing or a condition. 'stative' among the situation types is the first evidence; MACULA marks neither." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Sense ; rdfs:range xsd:string .

# ---- qualities: the adjectives' top level ---------------------------------

bom:QualityDimension a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Quality dimension" ;
    rdfs:comment "What an adjective measures: goodness, purity, strength, size, age, number, colour, wisdom. WordNet does not sort adjectives by meaning (its files are only 'all' and 'pertainym'), so this scheme is the one place the recommendation builds its own list, from the corpus's adjectives by frequency." .

bom:polarity a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "polarity" ;
    rdfs:comment "positive | negative | neutral, of a quality sense. Already useful: the polarity sequence that matched Psa 103:8-10 to 1Co 13:4-7 (JOURNAL, 2026-09-12)." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Sense ; rdfs:range xsd:string .

# ---- concepts and titles: named ideas and forms of address ---------------

bom:Concept a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Concept" ;
    rdfs:comment "An idea the text names and returns to: adoption, holiness, the fear of the LORD. A SKOS concept (it is a meaning, not a thing in the story), linked to the senses that express it (skos:related) and placed under a top category. The entity store's 1,388 `concept` records land here unchanged (docs/MEANING-ONTOLOGY.md, the crosswalk)." .

bom:Title a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bom:Category ;
    rdfs:label "Title" ;
    rdfs:comment "A form of address or office the text applies to someone: the Almighty, Abba, captain of the guard, an apostle of Jesus Christ. A title is a category a Predication applies (form: title), so the 74 `title` records keep their names and gain their citations." .

# ---- what the entity store already typed: kept, verbatim -------------------

bom:legacyType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "entity-store type" ;
    rdfs:comment "The `type` a record carried in ontology/entities/entities.jsonl (person, place, concept, image ...), kept verbatim on whatever it becomes here. Doug, 2026-09-28: 'we already have types which were not bad so I do not want to lose that progress.' Nothing is re-typed silently: the crosswalk's every move is a rule with a count (tools/meaning/crosswalk.py)." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

bom:legacyKind a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "entity-store kind" ;
    rdfs:comment "The record's attrs.kind (town, city, tribe, parable ...), kept verbatim; the controlled lists in ontology/schema/relationship_types.yaml become categories under their class (place_kinds under Place, group_kinds under Group, text_kinds under Saying)." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

bom:fromEntityType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "receives entity type" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a class: which entity-store type(s) land on it. The crosswalk, as data." ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Class ; rdfs:range xsd:string .

###############################################################################
#  LAYER 3 — REFERENT.
#
#  The things the text talks about as ONE thing across mentions: Abraham,
#  Jerusalem, the ark, the exodus. Typed by a SMALL closed set of classes
#  (the top categories, grouped), and only by the class nobody disputes.
#  Everything finer, and everything disputed, is a PREDICATION: an edge
#  saying who in the text called it what, where.
###############################################################################

bom:Entity a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Entity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Anything a word can be about. Abstract: every entity is one of the classes below, or — where the text's own categorizations conflict — left a bare Entity, with the conflict visible in its predications (PRINCIPLES 4)." .

# The five groups, then the classes. Each class names the WordNet
# lexicographer file(s) it is (bom:lexfile), so layer 2's categories land on
# exactly one class.

bom:Being a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Being" ; rdfs:comment "Living things and those the text treats as persons." .
bom:Thing a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Thing" ; rdfs:comment "Physical things that are not alive." .
bom:Setting a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Setting" ; rdfs:comment "Where and when." .
bom:Occurrent a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Occurrent" ; rdfs:comment "Things that happen, and conditions that hold." .
bom:Abstract a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Abstract" ; rdfs:comment "Things of the mind, of speech, of relation and measure." .

bom:lexfile a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "WordNet lexicographer file" ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Class ; rdfs:range xsd:string .

bom:PersonalBeing a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Being ;
    rdfs:label "Person or personal being" ;
    bom:lexfile "noun.person" ;
    rdfs:comment "Anyone the text treats as a person: a human, God, an angel, a spirit addressed or speaking. One class over all of them, with SUBCLASSES (Doug, 2026-09-28: 'there can be one personal being class for humans, God, and angels - but there should be sub-classes'). WordNet files God under noun.person too. 18.7% of categorized noun tokens." .

# ---- the subclasses, and how one being belongs to more than one -----------
#
# NOT DISJOINT, on purpose. Doug, 2026-09-28, on Jesus: "give Jesus
# membership in both Human and Deity classes which means the schema would
# need a way to accommodate that ... that would be my preference for Jesus
# Incarnate as the Bible clearly makes the case that he took on a form of a
# human."
#
# What keeps this on the right side of PRINCIPLES 4 ("a type is the one
# assertion in a record arriving with no citation"): membership of a
# subclass is never bare. Each rests on a bom:Classification naming the
# verses and who in the text says it, and shapes.ttl refuses a membership
# without one. A type WITH its citation is a claim that can be read and
# argued with, which is what the principle asks of a claim.

bom:Human a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:PersonalBeing ;
    rdfs:label "Human" ;
    rdfs:comment "A man, woman or child of the story." .
bom:Deity a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:PersonalBeing ;
    rdfs:label "Deity" ;
    rdfs:comment "God, and any god the text speaks of as a god — 'Dagon their god' (1Sa 5:7) is classified so by what the text says, with its speaker (the Philistines), not endorsed. The Classification's saidBy is what separates the two." .
bom:HeavenlyBeing a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:PersonalBeing ;
    rdfs:label "Heavenly being" ;
    rdfs:comment "Angels, messengers from God, cherubim, seraphim, the heavenly host." .
bom:Spirit a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:PersonalBeing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit" ;
    rdfs:comment "A spirit the text treats as acting or speaking: an unclean spirit, a demon, a lying spirit (1Ki 22:22)." .

bom:Classification a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Classification" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why an individual belongs to a subclass: the verses that make the case and who in the text says so. One per membership; an individual in two subclasses has two, each argued from its own verses (Jesus Incarnate: Human from Jhn 1:14, Php 2:7, 1Ti 2:5; Deity from Col 2:9)." .
bom:classifies a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Classification ; rdfs:range bom:Individual .
bom:inClass a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Classification ; rdfs:range rdfs:Class .
bom:statement a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Classification ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "What the text says that makes the case, quoted short." .

bom:assumedFormOf a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "was a form assumed by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Carried from the relation vocabulary (ontology/schema/relationship_types.yaml, Doug's decision 2026-09-01): the form is the subject, the one who assumed it the object — jesus_incarnate -assumedFormOf-> jesus (Php 2:6-8); the three men at Mamre -assumedFormOf-> the LORD (Gen 18:1-2). The incarnate node carries the Human membership the text argues for; the relation says whose form it is." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:Individual ; rdfs:range bom:Individual .
bom:Group a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Being ;
    rdfs:label "Group" ; bom:lexfile "noun.group" ;
    rdfs:comment "People taken as one: a tribe, a nation, a household, a congregation (ontology/schema group_kinds carries across as categories)." .
bom:Animal a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Being ;
    rdfs:label "Animal" ; bom:lexfile "noun.animal" ;
    rdfs:comment "Including the serpent of Genesis 3, typed by what Genesis says (identity.yaml): Rev 12:9's identification is a Predication, never a type." .
bom:Plant a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Being ;
    rdfs:label "Plant" ; bom:lexfile "noun.plant" .

bom:Artifact a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact" ; bom:lexfile "noun.artifact" ;
    rdfs:comment "Made things: sword, altar, ark, lamp, city wall." .
bom:BodyPart a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Body part" ; bom:lexfile "noun.body" .
bom:Food a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Food and drink" ; bom:lexfile "noun.food" .
bom:Substance a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Substance" ; bom:lexfile "noun.substance" ;
    rdfs:comment "Water, oil, blood, gold, clay." .
bom:NaturalObject a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Natural object" ; bom:lexfile "noun.object" ;
    rdfs:comment "The earth, a stone, a star, a rock." .

bom:Place a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Setting ;
    rdfs:label "Place" ; bom:lexfile "noun.location" ;
    rdfs:comment "place_kinds (city, town, region, mountain, river, well, sacred_site ...) carry across as categories under it." .
bom:Time a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Setting ;
    rdfs:label "Time" ; bom:lexfile "noun.time" .

bom:Event a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Occurrent ;
    rdfs:label "Event" ; bom:lexfile "noun.event noun.process" ;
    rdfs:comment "A happening as a thing: the flood, the exodus, a feast." .
bom:Act a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Occurrent ;
    rdfs:label "Act" ; bom:lexfile "noun.act" ;
    rdfs:comment "A doing named as a noun: sacrifice, war, judgement." .
bom:State a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Occurrent ;
    rdfs:label "State" ; bom:lexfile "noun.state" ;
    rdfs:comment "Peace, sickness, barrenness." .
bom:Phenomenon a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Occurrent ;
    rdfs:label "Phenomenon" ; bom:lexfile "noun.phenomenon" ;
    rdfs:comment "Light, fire, wind, rain, thunder." .

bom:Feeling a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling" ; bom:lexfile "noun.feeling" .
bom:Cognition a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Cognition" ; bom:lexfile "noun.cognition" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wisdom, knowledge, faith (as WordNet files it)." .
bom:Communication a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Communication" ; bom:lexfile "noun.communication" ;
    rdfs:comment "Word, law, name, song, letter — as things; a speech ACT is structure (kinds.json Communication)." .
bom:Attribute a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Attribute" ; bom:lexfile "noun.attribute" ;
    rdfs:comment "Holiness, righteousness, strength, height." .
bom:Quantity a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Quantity" ; bom:lexfile "noun.quantity" .
bom:Possession a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Possession" ; bom:lexfile "noun.possession" ;
    rdfs:comment "Inheritance, wages, silver as money." .
bom:Relation a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Relation" ; bom:lexfile "noun.relation" .
bom:Shape a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Shape" ; bom:lexfile "noun.shape" .
bom:Motive a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Abstract ;
    rdfs:label "Motive" ; bom:lexfile "noun.motive" .

# Disjointness only where it is safe to assert: a being is not a setting.
# NOT asserted: literal vs figurative (that is per occurrence, never per
# thing), nor anything among Being's classes that the text itself crosses.
# NOR DISJOINTNESS AS OWL (OWL evaluation Phase 0, Doug 2026-10-13: "Move
# disjointness"). The four axioms that stood here -- Being and Thing each
# disjoint with Setting and Abstract -- made the whole graph inconsistent the
# moment the text crossed them (Noah's ark, an object and a place; the
# temple; the Word of John 1), halting a reasoner instead of naming one node.
# The intent is kept closed-world, as a report on the node: bosh:OneTopClass
# in shapes.ttl.

# ---- individuals and their worlds ------------------------------------------

bom:Individual a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Individual" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE referent across its mentions: Abraham, Jerusalem, Goliath's sword. Typed by one class above (the least disputed). The 8,416 records of ontology/entities/entities.jsonl are the first candidates; their `type` maps onto the classes (person->PersonalBeing, place->Place, group->Group, creature->Animal, object->Artifact, event->Event, covenant->Communication?), and 'being' and 'image', which make claims, become predications and figures." .

bom:mentions a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "mentions" ;
    rdfs:comment "An occurrence refers to an individual: a name, a pronoun resolved by S5 (`refers_to`), a description. The structure's refers_to becomes this where its target is an individual." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Occurrence ; rdfs:range bom:Individual .

bom:World a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "World" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a referent exists for the text. The story world, or a TOLD world: the sower of a parable, the ram and goat of Daniel 8, the beast of Revelation 13 are not individuals of the story world, and nothing about them transfers there except what the text says (the interpretation it gives)." .
bom:StoryWorld a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:World .
bom:ToldWorld a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:World ;
    rdfs:comment "Opened by a parable, dream, vision or sign-act (the old graph's text_kinds): a structure-side Communication whose content is a told world (BACKLOG, 2026-09-25)." .

bom:inWorld a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain bom:Individual ; rdfs:range bom:World .

bom:Saying a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Saying" ;
    rdfs:comment "A memorable expression the text uses, recorded as a unit: 'a still small voice', 'I lay a stone in Zion', 'a ransom for many'. The entity store's `text` records (2,305) land here with their kind; those whose kind is parable, dream, vision or sign also OPEN a told world (bom:opens)." ;
    bom:fromEntityType "text" .
bom:opens a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Saying ; rdfs:range bom:ToldWorld .

# The crosswalk, as data on the classes (docs/MEANING-ONTOLOGY.md, part 7)
bom:PersonalBeing bom:fromEntityType "person", "being" .
bom:Place bom:fromEntityType "place" .
bom:Group bom:fromEntityType "group" .
bom:Animal bom:fromEntityType "creature" .
bom:Artifact bom:fromEntityType "object" .
bom:Event bom:fromEntityType "event", "observance" .
bom:Communication bom:fromEntityType "covenant" .
bom:Concept bom:fromEntityType "concept" .
bom:Title bom:fromEntityType "title" .
bom:Image bom:fromEntityType "image" .
bom:Predication bom:fromEntityType "assessment" .

bom:toldBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "told by" ;
    rdfs:comment "A told world -> the structure node (a Turn, a Writing's part) that tells it." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:ToldWorld .

# ---- predication: how the text itself categorizes an individual ----------

bom:Predication a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Predication" ;
    rdfs:comment "Somebody in the text says X is (or is called) a Y: 'the LORD is a man of war' (Exo 15:3), 'my Lord and my God' (Jhn 20:28), 'that ancient serpent, who is called the devil' (Rev 12:9). The saying is the fact (PRINCIPLES 2). What kind of thing X is, is answered by collecting these — the text's own categorization, every one cited — rather than by one type." .

bom:predicationOf a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Predication ; rdfs:range bom:Individual .
bom:predicatedCategory a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Predication ; rdfs:range bom:Category ;
    rdfs:comment "Said to be a kind of thing: 'a man', 'a prophet', 'a king'." .
bom:predicatedIdentity a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Predication ; rdfs:range bom:Individual ;
    rdfs:comment "Said to be another individual: 'who is called the devil'. NOT owl:sameAs: an identity claim made in the text is a relation to record, never a merge (identity.yaml)." .
# NO rdfs:domain on saidBy: it is used on a Predication or a Classification,
# and with domain Predication RDFS inference made every Classification a
# Predication (caught by tools/meaning/check.py, 2026-09-28, the second time a
# shared property's domain did this). The published comment below says what it
# is; this note says why it has no domain (moved out of the published text,
# 2026-10-14, Phase 0).
bom:saidBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:comment "Who says it: a speaker, the narrator, or the author of a writing; it applies to a predication or a classification." .
bom:form a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Predication ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "How the text says it: copula ('X is Y'), apposition ('David the king'), name ('called'), vocative ('O LORD'), epithet." .
bom:evidence a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "evidence" ;
    rdfs:comment "The occurrences or clause (structure ids) the fact rests on. Required on every predication, figure and reading." .

###############################################################################
#  LAYER 4 — FIGURE.  Doug's question: "a literal word might have a symbolic
#  meaning — 'sword' and 'sword of the Spirit' ... Light is a common image."
#
#  The word never changes category: μάχαιρα is a sword (an Artifact) in
#  Mat 26:51 and in Eph 6:17. What differs is the USE. So a figure is a node
#  on the occurrence, naming the image (vehicle), what it is said of (tenor),
#  how the text marks it, and whether the tenor is STATED by the text or READ
#  by someone. Images collect their uses by vehicle; they never chain.
###############################################################################

bom:usage a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "usage" ;
    rdfs:comment "Of an occurrence: bom:Literal, bom:Figurative, or bom:Unmarked. No default (PRINCIPLES 18): an occurrence is Figurative only where a FigurativeUse exists, Literal only where something marks it so, and otherwise Unmarked — which is most words, and says nothing." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Occurrence ; rdfs:range bom:Usage .
bom:Usage a rdfs:Class .
bom:Literal a bom:Usage ; rdfs:label "literal" .
bom:Figurative a bom:Usage ; rdfs:label "figurative" .
bom:Unmarked a bom:Usage ; rdfs:label "unmarked" .
bom:Sign a bom:Usage ; rdfs:label "sign (literal and figurative at once)" ;
    rdfs:comment "A real thing in the scene that ALSO stands for something, where the TEXT says so: the bow in the cloud is a bow and 'the sign of the covenant' (Gen 9:13); the stones from the Jordan are stones and 'a sign among you' (Jos 4:6). Taken from a Copilot thread's 'dual use' (Doug, 2026-09-28: 'use what makes sense, only'); its gate is left behind: repetition, extra attention, contrast or 'cultural meaning' do NOT make a use a sign — Exodus repeats the tabernacle's boards for chapters and they are boards (PRINCIPLES 18, 49). Those signals may rank candidates for reading; they decide nothing. A Sign carries a FigurativeUse with tenorBasis 'stated'." .

bom:Contested a bom:Usage ; rdfs:label "contested (readers divide)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Readers divide on whether the use is literal or figurative, and the text does not settle it: 'This is My body' (Mat 26:26) — the bread IS his body, or a sign of it; Ezekiel's temple (Ezk 40–48) — a temple to be built, or God's presence among his people. Doug, 2026-09-28: 'I would like to support either reading.' The graph decides nothing: each side is a UsageReading credited to its reader, and a view can take the graph as either reads it (PRINCIPLES 1, as the Trinity texts are held: both kept, neither resolved). NOT the same as Unmarked (no one has claimed anything) and NOT used where the text itself settles it — 'He was speaking about the temple of His body' (Jhn 2:21) is a stated figure." .

bom:UsageReading a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Reading ;
    rdfs:label "Usage reading" ;
    rdfs:comment "One reader's answer to 'literal or figurative?' at one contested use, with what it reads the thing as. Several may stand on one occurrence; all are kept." .
bom:readsUsageOf a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:UsageReading ; rdfs:range bow:Occurrence .
bom:readsUsage a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:UsageReading ; rdfs:range bom:Usage ;
    rdfs:comment "Literal, Figurative or Sign — never Contested or Unmarked." .
bom:readsAs a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:UsageReading ;
    rdfs:comment "What this reader takes it to be: 'his body', 'a sign of his body given', 'a temple to be built', 'God's presence among his people'." .

# ---- conventional figures: Doug's heart ------------------------------------
#
# Doug, 2026-09-28: "heart is an interesting case that is on the edge of
# symbol and tangible thing in the way that it is normally used." It is not
# a figure made fresh in a verse (like "the LORD is my shepherd"); the
# language has already made it: לֵב is the organ AND the inner self, and the
# inner self is what almost every use means. So it is a SENSE of its own,
# grown from the literal one — not a FigurativeUse on each occurrence, which
# would flag half the Bible.
bom:extendedFrom a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "extended from" ;
    rdfs:comment "Sense -> the sense it grew from by a CONVENTIONAL figure: heart (the inner self) from heart (the organ); face (presence) from face; arm (power) from arm; flesh (the fallen self, in Paul) from flesh; way (conduct) from way; tongue and lips (speech) from the organs. The reading of 285 concept records found 39 such, and they are decided per word, with Doug." ;
    rdfs:domain bow:Sense ; rdfs:range bow:Sense .
bom:figureKind a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:comment "On an extendedFrom link: metonymy (the arm for the power it wields), metaphor (the heart for the inner self), synecdoche (flesh for the whole person)." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

bom:FigurativeUse a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Figurative use" ;
    rdfs:comment "One place the text speaks in an image." .

bom:figureAt a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The occurrence (or clause) where the image is used." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse .
bom:vehicle a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "vehicle (the image)" ;
    rdfs:comment "What is pictured: the sense (and so its category) of the image word — sword, lamp, shepherd, rock. Richards's 'vehicle'; conceptual metaphor theory's source domain." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse ; rdfs:range bow:Sense .
bom:tenor a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "tenor (what it is said of)" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the image is applied to: an individual ('the LORD'), a stated meaning ('the word of God', Eph 6:17), or absent where the text does not say. On a FigurativeUse when the text STATES it; on a SymbolReading when a reader supplies it. Deliberately NO rdfs:domain: with domain FigurativeUse, RDFS inference turned every reader's SymbolReading into a figure of the text — the first check of this schema caught exactly that (2026-09-28)." .
bom:tenorBasis a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:comment "stated — the text itself says what the image is (a copula, 'which is', an interpreter in a vision); read — a reader supplies it, and then the tenor is a Reading (layer 5) with its reader named." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse ; rdfs:range xsd:string .
bom:mode a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "mode" ;
    rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse ; rdfs:range bom:FigureMode .
bom:marker a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "marked by" ;
    rdfs:comment "What in the form shows it: 'category clash in a copular clause', 'comparative כְּ / ὡς', 'relative which-is', 'genitive of apposition', 'an explanation in a told world'. A use found by a rule that judges (the clash rule) carries bom:status 'flagged' until read (PRINCIPLES 49)." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse .

bom:FigureMode a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    # admitted as the text marks them: kinds.json's rule, a reading is not a kind
    rdfs:comment "The kind of figure of speech a figurative use is: metaphor, simile, metonymy, synecdoche, personification, hyperbole, symbol in a told world, or sign-act, admitted as the text marks them." .
bom:metaphor a bom:FigureMode ; skos:prefLabel "metaphor" ;
    skos:definition "The image said to BE its tenor: 'the LORD is my shepherd'." .
bom:simile a bom:FigureMode ; skos:prefLabel "simile" ;
    skos:definition "The image said to be LIKE its tenor, with a word that says so: כְּ, ὡς, ὥσπερ." .
bom:metonymy a bom:FigureMode ; skos:prefLabel "metonymy" ;
    skos:definition "A thing named by what goes with it: 'the sword' for war." .
bom:personification a bom:FigureMode ; skos:prefLabel "personification" ;
    skos:definition "A non-person spoken of as acting or speaking as a person: 'wisdom calls aloud' (Pro 1:20)." .
bom:symbol a bom:FigureMode ; skos:prefLabel "symbol in a told world" ;
    skos:definition "A thing seen in a dream or vision that the text (or a reader) says stands for something: the ram is the kings of Media and Persia (Dan 8:20, stated)." .

bom:Image a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Image" ;
    rdfs:comment "A recurring vehicle as one node: 'the sword', 'light', 'the shepherd'. It gathers its figurative uses and nothing more. Two images are NOT related because their tenors coincide (seed -> word of God <- sword: PRINCIPLES 17)." .
bom:ofImage a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:FigurativeUse ; rdfs:range bom:Image .

# DISTINCT NODES FOR THE THING AND ITS IMAGE — carried from the old graph.
# Doug, 2026-09-28: "this is why we need distinct nodes for the literal and
# symbolic uses of words like the sword", and "old graph used 'image of'".
# The old relation vocabulary (ontology/schema/relationship_types.yaml) has
# both edges, and they keep their names and meaning here:

bom:drawnFrom a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "is drawn from" ;
    skos:altLabel "is used as an image (inverse)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Image -> the tangible thing of the same word: the sword image -> the sword (a weapon: its sense, or an individual sword). Old `drawn_from`: 'The lion of 1 Peter 5:8 and the lion Daniel was shut in with are NOT ONE NODE: one is a figure, one is an animal in a den, and this edge says they share a word without saying they share' anything more." ;
    rdfs:domain bom:Image .

bom:drawsOn a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "draws on the image of" ;
    skos:altLabel "is the image behind (inverse)" ;
    rdfs:comment "A saying, title or predication -> the image it is built on: 'the whole armour of God' draws on the breastplate, the helmet, the shield and the sword (Eph 6:11-17). Old `draws_on` (240 edges): 'a lexical claim and nothing more' — it records that the saying is built on the word, not what the image means (that would be a Reading)." ;
    rdfs:range bom:Image .

bom:ImageField a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Image field" ;
    rdfs:comment "Images used TOGETHER in one passage, as one picture: shepherd, sheep, pasture, still waters, rod and staff (Psa 23; plan 4.4). A field is found by co-occurrence inside a structure group, with the group as evidence — never by similarity of meaning." .
bom:inField a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:Image ; rdfs:range bom:ImageField .

###############################################################################
#  LAYER 5 — READING.  Themes, typology, symbolic meanings, patterns: what
#  readers see over the text. Kept, valued, and never typed onto a node.
###############################################################################

# Readers include the site's owner and model runs (with their dates). The
# earlier published comment named him and quoted a private ruling on a source
# credited outside the graph; replaced 2026-10-14 (Phase 0) -- the ruling
# itself is kept in the project's records, not in the published vocabulary.
bom:Reader a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prov:Agent ;
    rdfs:comment "Whoever offers a reading of the text, such as a Bible's editors, a commentator or a model run; a reading always names its reader." .

bom:Reading a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Reading" ;
    rdfs:comment "One reader's claim about the text, with the reader (prov:wasAttributedTo) and the evidence it points at. Readings may disagree and all are kept (PRINCIPLES 21). The BSB pronoun layer (S6, reading: 'BSB') is the first built instance of this." .

bom:ThemeStamp a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Reading ;
    # the reader's scheme here is ontology/interest-taxonomy.json
    rdfs:comment "A reader places a passage under a theme; themes are concepts in the reader's own scheme, never classes and never passed on to what the passage mentions." .
bom:theme a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:ThemeStamp ; rdfs:range skos:Concept .

# DEPRECATED IN 1.0.0 (Doug, 2026-10-14: "deprecate them"). Never used by
# the graph, and a class named for typology in the published vocabulary said
# the graph meant to hold typological readings -- one tradition's way of
# reading, at odds with taking no side where traditions differ. Where the
# text itself draws a comparison it is structure; parallels it does not state
# are shown without a claim of intent. Addresses kept (the versioning rule).
bom:TypologyReading a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Reading ;
    rdfs:label "typology reading" ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:comment "Deprecated, never used. Where the text itself draws a comparison between one thing and another, the graph records it as the text's own statement; parallels the text does not state are shown without a claim of intent. The address is kept so that nothing that cites it breaks." .
bom:prefigures a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:TypologyReading ;
    rdfs:label "prefigures" ; owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:comment "Deprecated with TypologyReading, never used." .
bom:prefigured a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:TypologyReading ;
    rdfs:label "prefigured" ; owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:comment "Deprecated with TypologyReading, never used." .

bom:SymbolReading a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bom:Reading ;
    # the example it was written from: the sword from the mouth (Rev 1:16) read as the word of God
    rdfs:comment "A reader supplies the meaning of an image that the text does not state; it hangs on the figurative use and is not part of the text's own use." .
bom:readsTenorOf a rdf:Property ; rdfs:domain bom:SymbolReading ; rdfs:range bom:FigurativeUse .

###############################################################################
#  LABELS AND DEFINITIONS, 1.0.0 (OWL evaluation Phase 0). Drafted by Claude
#  Sonnet where a term had none; reviewed and approved by Doug Egge,
#  2026-10-14 ('approve the rest'). AI-written wording, excluded from the
#  copyright claim; the terms and their arrangement are his.
###############################################################################

bom:BodyPart skos:definition "A part of a human or animal body, such as a bone, the womb or a rib." .
bom:Feeling skos:definition "A feeling or emotion named as a thing, such as fear or love." .
bom:Food skos:definition "Food or drink named as a thing, such as grain, almonds or a prepared dish." .
bom:Motive skos:definition "A reason or drive that moves someone to act, named as a thing." .
bom:Plant skos:definition "A plant, such as thistles, reeds or wheat." .
bom:Quantity skos:definition "An amount or measure named as a thing." .
bom:Relation skos:definition "A relation between things or events, named as a thing." .
bom:Shape skos:definition "The shape or form of a thing, named as a thing." .
bom:Time skos:definition "A point or stretch of time, such as the middle of the night." .
bom:Literal skos:definition "A word occurrence taken at face value; it is applied only where something marks the use as literal, never by default." .
bom:Figurative skos:definition "A word occurrence that the text uses in an image; it applies only where a figurative use stands at that occurrence." .
bom:Unmarked skos:definition "A word occurrence that nothing marks as literal or figurative, which says nothing either way and describes most words." .
bom:Usage rdfs:label "usage class" ;
    skos:definition "How a word occurrence is being used: literal, figurative, a sign, contested between readers, or unmarked." .
bom:StoryWorld rdfs:label "story world" ;
    skos:definition "The world of the story itself, where the individuals the text narrates exist, as distinct from a told world opened by a parable, dream, vision or sign." .
bom:ToldWorld rdfs:label "told world" .
bom:classifies rdfs:label "classifies" ;
    skos:definition "From a classification to the individual that it places in a subclass." .
bom:inClass rdfs:label "in class" ;
    skos:definition "From a classification to the class that the individual is placed in." .
bom:predicationOf rdfs:label "predication of" ;
    skos:definition "From a predication to the individual that the text says something about." .
bom:readsAs rdfs:label "reads as" .
bom:readsUsage rdfs:label "reads usage as" .
bom:readsUsageOf rdfs:label "reads usage of" ;
    skos:definition "From a usage reading to the word occurrence whose literal or figurative use it answers for." .
bom:readsTenorOf rdfs:label "reads tenor of" ;
    skos:definition "From a symbol reading to the figurative use whose tenor the reader supplies." .
bom:theme rdfs:label "theme" ;
    skos:definition "On a theme stamp, the theme, a concept in the reader's scheme, that the passage is placed under." .
bom:inField rdfs:label "in field" ;
    skos:definition "From an image to the image field, the set of images used together in one passage, that it belongs to." .
bom:inWorld rdfs:label "in world" ;
    skos:definition "From an individual to the world, the story or a told world, in which it exists for the text." .
bom:opens rdfs:label "opens" ;
    skos:definition "From a saying, such as a parable, dream, vision or sign, to the told world that it opens." .
bom:ofImage rdfs:label "of image" ;
    skos:definition "From a figurative use to the image whose vehicle it uses." .
bom:form rdfs:label "form" .
bom:figureKind rdfs:label "figure kind" .
bom:statement rdfs:label "statement" .
bom:tenorBasis rdfs:label "tenor basis" .
bom:predicatedCategory rdfs:label "predicated category" .
bom:predicatedIdentity rdfs:label "predicated identity" .
bom:lexfile skos:definition "The WordNet lexicographer file or files whose words a class gathers." .
bom:mode skos:definition "The kind of figure a figurative use is, such as metaphor, simile, metonymy, personification or symbol." .
bom:situationType skos:definition "The kind of happening a verb sense names, such as motion, communication or perception." .
bow:hasSense rdfs:label "has sense" ;
    skos:definition "From a lexeme to a sense that it can carry." .
bow:ofLexeme skos:definition "From a word occurrence to the dictionary word, the lexeme, that it is an instance of." .
# the labels of the five whose published comments were replaced (same review)
bom:SymbolReading rdfs:label "symbol reading" .
bom:ThemeStamp rdfs:label "theme stamp" .
bom:Reader rdfs:label "reader" .
bom:FigureMode rdfs:label "figure mode" .
bom:saidBy rdfs:label "said by" .

###############################################################################
#  THE FILTER AS DATA: strongest by default, the rest on request (plan 8.1f,
#  2026-10-19; Doug: "Card words + navigator"). A card's words and the
#  navigator's ties, each with its strength as a W3C Data Quality Vocabulary
#  measurement against a metric declared here, and the policy that turns a
#  strength into what a view shows first. Nothing is deleted: everything is
#  published, and the shown view is a declared subset.
###############################################################################
@prefix dqv: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dqv#> .

bom:CardWord a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "card word" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Hebrew, Aramaic or Greek word that a record holds as one of its words: the record (bom:card), the dictionary word (bom:word), the ground on which it holds it (bom:wordBasis), its strength where it has one, and whether a reader is shown it first (bom:tier)." .
bom:card a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "card" ; rdfs:domain bom:CardWord ;
    rdfs:comment "The record whose word this is." .
bom:word a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "word" ; rdfs:domain bom:CardWord ; rdfs:range bow:Lexeme ;
    rdfs:comment "The dictionary word, the lexeme." .
bom:wordBasis a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "word basis" ; rdfs:domain bom:CardWord ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "Why the record holds the word: anchored (its renderings render the record's name), anchored to a record named by a phrase (no single word carries a phrase), the word a verse the record cites uses, or the same root or a word derived from one of its words." .
bom:Tier a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:label "tier" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether a view shows a thing first or on request, under the filter policy." .
bom:default a bom:Tier ; rdfs:label "shown by default" .
bom:onRequest a bom:Tier ; rdfs:label "shown on request" .
bom:tier a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "tier" ; rdfs:range bom:Tier ;
    rdfs:comment "The tier the filter policy gives a thing from its strength." .

bom:Tie a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:label "tie" ;
    rdfs:comment "A relation between two records as the knowledge graph navigator draws it: the two records, the relation type, and the tie's strength." .
bom:source a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "source" ; rdfs:domain bom:Tie .
bom:target a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "target" ; rdfs:domain bom:Tie .
bom:relation a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "relation" ; rdfs:domain bom:Tie ;
    rdfs:comment "The relation type, from the relation vocabulary." .
bom:label a rdf:Property ; rdfs:label "label" ; rdfs:domain bom:Tie ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "A statement's own verb, where the relation is a statement the text makes." .

bom:salience a dqv:Dimension ; rdfs:label "salience" ;
    rdfs:comment "How strongly the text supports showing a thing first." .
bom:cardWordShare a dqv:Metric ; rdfs:label "card word share" ; dqv:inDimension bom:salience ;
    rdfs:comment "The share of a word's renderings in the text that render the record's name, from 0 to 1. A word at or above 0.15 is shown by default; a word a cited verse uses is shown whatever its share." .
bom:navigatorTieStrength a dqv:Metric ; rdfs:label "navigator tie strength" ; dqv:inDimension bom:salience ;
    rdfs:comment "A certainty factor (explicit 1, implied 0.8, disputed 0.6, conjectured 0.55) times the sum of 0.35 times the relation type's weight and 1.1 times log2 of one plus the verses that name both records, chapters naming both counting half." .
bom:navigatorFloor a dqv:Metric ; rdfs:label "navigator floor" ; dqv:inDimension bom:salience ;
    rdfs:comment "For a record as the navigator's centre, the lowest tie strength that keeps its first view to 21 records, the centre included; ties of equal strength are kept or left out together." .
bom:filterPolicy a dqv:QualityPolicy ; rdfs:label "filter policy" ;
    rdfs:comment "Strongest by default, the rest on request; nothing is deleted. A card word is shown by default at a card word share of 0.15 or more, or when a verse the record cites uses it. The navigator first draws the ties around its centre whose strength is at least the centre's floor. Certainty is published with every claim and hides nothing; it lowers a tie's strength. A view chooses whether to apply the policy." .
